2. **In tests**, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (from the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(365)); // move time forward
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

- `TimeProvider` is **built into .NET 8+** — no wrapper interface to maintain.
- The `timeProvider` parameter defaults to `null`, so **existing callers don't break** (`new InvoiceCalculator()` still works).
- `FakeTimeProvider` lets you set and advance time deterministically, eliminating flakiness.
